{
	"id": "g3.maths.times_tables",
	"lesson": [
		{
			"text": "Quick memory: 4 × 5 = 20 is 4 rows of 5. Turn the array round: 5 × 4 = 20 too. Every fact you know is two facts!",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "array",
				"rows": 4,
				"cols": 5
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "The times grid holds every fact up to 12 × 12. Find 7 × 8: go along row 7 to column 8. It is 56. There are 144 squares, but many are friends you already know.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "times_grid",
				"rows": 12,
				"cols": 12,
				"r0": 1,
				"c0": 1,
				"op": "×",
				"shade_row": 7,
				"shade_col": 8
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Patterns help. The 2s, 4s, 6s, 8s and 10s are all even. The 5s end in 5 or 0. In the 9s, the digits add up to 9: 18, 27, 36.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "times_grid",
				"rows": 1,
				"cols": 10,
				"r0": 9,
				"c0": 1,
				"op": "×"
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Tricks for tricky tables: × 4 is double, then double again. × 8 is double three times. × 9 is × 10, then take away the number once: 9 × 7 = 70 − 7 = 63."
		},
		{
			"text": "The 11s and 12s: 11 × 6 = 60 + 6 = 66. For 12 × 6, split 12 into 10 and 2: 10 × 6 = 60 and 2 × 6 = 12. 60 + 12 = 72.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "area_model",
				"cols": [
					"10",
					"2"
				],
				"rows": [
					"6"
				],
				"cells": [
					[
						"60",
						"12"
					]
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Every times fact gives division facts too: 7 × 8 = 56, so 56 ÷ 8 = 7 and 56 ÷ 7 = 8. Next: tricks that make multiplying even easier.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "times_grid",
				"rows": 3,
				"cols": 4,
				"r0": 6,
				"c0": 6,
				"op": "×",
				"shade_row": 7
			}
		}
	],
